from common import lesson, lab, cite, run, code

LESSONS = [
lesson(2,1,'names-and-objects','Names bind to objects; mutation is visible through every name',
 'Python has no immutability by default and no ownership. A name is a label on an object, and whoever holds a label can change the object.',
 '''<h2 id="the-model">The model you must replace</h2>
<p>In Elixir, <code>list = [1, 2]</code> followed by <code>other = list</code> gives two names for a value that nobody can change; <code>[0 | list]</code> is a new list. In Rust, <code>let other = list;</code> moves ownership and the compiler stops you using <code>list</code> again. Python does neither. Assignment binds a name to an object; the object has an identity, a type, and a value, and only the value can change '''+cite('py-datamodel')+'''. Two names bound to one mutable object are two doors into the same room.</p>
<p>The language reference puts it in one sentence: numbers, strings and tuples are immutable, while dictionaries and lists are mutable. Add to the mutable side everything that matters in this course: NumPy arrays, torch tensors, <code>nn.Module</code>s, and the dictionaries that hold activation caches.</p>
'''+run('python3 labs/examples/names_and_objects.py')+'''
<h2 id="read-the-output">Read the output line by line</h2>
<p><code>same = scores</code> creates no list. <code>same.append(0.9)</code> mutates the one list, so <code>scores</code> sees it, and <code>scores is same</code> is true because <code>is</code> compares identity, the thing <code>id()</code> returns. <code>list(scores)</code> builds a new list with the same elements, so appending to <code>fresh</code> leaves <code>scores</code> alone; <code>is</code> is now false even though the first three elements are equal. That is the whole difference between <code>is</code> and <code>==</code>: identity versus value.</p>
<p>The shallow copy of <code>run</code> is a new dict, but its <code>"prompts"</code> value is the same list object as the original's. Appending through the copy changes the original. <code>copy.deepcopy</code> copies recursively and breaks the sharing. You will meet exactly this when you copy a config dict, edit a nested list of hook names, and discover the original config changed too.</p>
<p>The tuple line shows what immutability looks like when you do have it: <code>t + (3,)</code> is a new object, <code>t</code> is untouched. The integer line shows the same for numbers, and explains why <code>b += 1</code> did not change <code>a</code>: there is no integer to mutate, so <code>+=</code> rebinds <code>b</code> to a new int. On a list, <code>b += [1]</code> mutates in place. Same operator, two behaviours, decided by the type.</p>
<h2 id="default-trap">The mutable default argument</h2>
<p><code>def record(result, log=[])</code> evaluates <code>[]</code> once, when the function is defined, and every call without a <code>log</code> argument shares that one list. The output shows the second call returning both results. The idiom is <code>log=None</code> and <code>log = [] if log is None else log</code> inside the body. The dataclass module raises an error for a list default for exactly this reason, which lesson 05 shows.</p>
<h2 id="when-it-matters">Where this bites in model work</h2>
<p>A hook that stores <code>output</code> in a dict stores a reference to the tensor the model is still using; if a later in-place operation touches that tensor, your cache changes after the fact. Lesson 13's hooks call <code>.detach().clone()</code> for that reason. A function that "returns" a modified dict by mutating its argument changes the caller's object; the eval-pipeline spec forbids mutating inputs precisely so results stay comparable. And <code>a = b = torch.zeros(3)</code> gives two names for one tensor, not two tensors.</p>
<p>The habit from Rust that transfers best is asking "who else holds this?" before mutating. Python does not answer the question for you, so answer it by convention: functions that take data return new data unless their name says otherwise (<code>sort()</code> mutates and returns <code>None</code>; <code>sorted()</code> returns a new list), and anything you cache gets copied on the way in.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p>The eval-pipeline tests check that <code>score_ticket</code> and <code>summarize_run</code> do not mutate their inputs. That is not pedantry: a grader that normalises a prediction in place changes the record that gets logged, and the log no longer shows what the model said.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Using <code>is</code> to compare values. <code>x is 5</code> and <code>s is "gpt2"</code> happen to work for small ints and short strings because the interpreter caches them, and silently fail elsewhere. Use <code>==</code> for values and reserve <code>is</code> for <code>None</code>, <code>True</code>, <code>False</code>, and genuine identity questions.</p></div>''',
 'A function receives a list of result dicts and must return the same list with a <code>"passed"</code> field added to each. A colleague writes <code>for r in results: r["passed"] = grade(r)</code> and returns <code>results</code>. What has changed for the caller, and is that acceptable?',
 'Every dict the caller holds now has a new key, because the loop mutated the shared objects; the list itself was also returned unchanged in identity. Whether that is acceptable depends on the contract. If the caller logged the raw results first, the log is now stale. The safer default is <code>[{**r, "passed": grade(r)} for r in results]</code>: new dicts, originals untouched, and the function name can then promise it.',
 lab('Without running it, predict the output of each line of <code>labs/examples/names_and_objects.py</code>, writing your predictions down. Then run it and score yourself. For each miss, write one sentence on which rule you had wrong.',
 'The common misses are the shallow copy sharing the inner list, and <code>b += 1</code> not affecting <code>a</code>. Both come from the same rule: assignment and augmented assignment act on names and on the object\'s type; they never copy.',
 ['python3 labs/examples/names_and_objects.py']), ['py-datamodel'], 'identity aliasing mutation copy deepcopy is equals default argument'),

lesson(3,1,'records-and-errors','Dicts, lists, JSON and JSONL, and exceptions as the error channel',
 'The records you will read all day are dicts and lists that came from JSON. Errors arrive as exceptions, not as a second return value.',
 '''<h2 id="records">A record is a dict</h2>
<p>An eval case, a hook configuration, a run manifest, a Hugging Face config: in Python they are all <code>dict</code>s, usually produced by <code>json.loads</code>. Keys are strings, values are strings, numbers, booleans, <code>None</code>, lists, or more dicts. Access is <code>row["id"]</code>, which raises <code>KeyError</code> when the key is absent, or <code>row.get("id")</code>, which returns <code>None</code>. Prefer the first in loaders: a missing field is an error you want to see, not a <code>None</code> that travels three functions before something divides by it.</p>
'''+run('python3 labs/examples/records_and_errors.py')+'''
<h2 id="json">JSON round trips and the three traps</h2>
<p><code>json.dumps</code> turns <code>True</code> into <code>true</code> and <code>None</code> into <code>null</code>, and <code>json.loads</code> turns them back '''+cite('py-json')+'''. Dict equality ignores key order, as the second line shows. Then come the traps the eval-pipeline spec is written around.</p>
<p><strong>One: <code>1 == True</code>.</strong> <code>bool</code> is a subclass of <code>int</code>, so a prediction with <code>"urgent": 1</code> compares equal to a reference with <code>"urgent": true</code>. An exact-schema scorer must check <code>type(value) is type(expected)</code> as well as equality, and the output shows that <code>type(1) is bool</code> is false while <code>type(True) is bool</code> is true. This is the false pass that <code>score_ticket</code>'s tests exist to catch.</p>
<p><strong>Two: duplicate keys.</strong> The json module accepts repeated names in an object and keeps the last value by default. The documented remedy is <code>object_pairs_hook</code>, a function called with the ordered list of (key, value) pairs for every object at every depth; the example rejects the duplicate with a <code>ValueError</code>. <code>load_cases</code> needs this, because a case file with <code>"reference"</code> written twice would otherwise grade against whichever came last, with the row count unchanged.</p>
<p><strong>Three: physical line numbers.</strong> A JSONL file is one JSON value per line '''+cite('py-json')+'''. The loop uses <code>enumerate(f, start=1)</code> so the number it reports is the line in the file, and it skips blank lines after counting them: line 3 holds the second record and line 4 holds the bad one. <code>JSONDecodeError</code> is a subclass of <code>ValueError</code> and carries <code>msg</code>, <code>lineno</code> and <code>colno</code>, but note that its <code>lineno</code> is relative to the string you passed, which is one line; the file line number is yours to track.</p>
<h2 id="exceptions">Exceptions are the error channel</h2>
<p>In Elixir you return <code>{:ok, value}</code> or <code>{:error, reason}</code> and pattern-match; in Rust you return <code>Result</code> and use <code>?</code>. Python raises. An exception is an object that unwinds the stack until an <code>except</code> clause whose type matches catches it. Uncaught, it ends the program with a traceback, which is the correct behaviour for a bug and the wrong one for an expected condition like a missing file.</p>
<p>The idiom is to catch the narrowest type that means what you expect. <code>FileNotFoundError</code> is a different class from the <code>ValueError</code> you raise for bad data, and the spec for <code>load_cases</code> requires keeping them distinct so a caller can tell "no file" from "bad file". <code>try / except / else / finally</code> reads as: attempt, handle this failure, run this only on success, and always run this. The last clause is where hooks get removed in lesson 13.</p>
<p>Raise with a message that names the location: <code>raise ValueError(f"line {lineno}: duplicate case id {case_id!r}")</code>. The <code>!r</code> asks for the <code>repr</code>, which shows quotes and makes a trailing space visible. Re-raising with context, <code>raise ValueError(...) from exc</code>, keeps the original traceback attached.</p>
<h2 id="lists">Lists, tuples, sets, and the operations you will reach for</h2>
<p>A <code>list</code> is an ordered, mutable array of references: <code>append</code>, indexing, slicing (<code>rows[1:3]</code>, a new list), and <code>len</code>. A <code>tuple</code> is the immutable version and is what shapes are. A <code>set</code> holds unique hashable values, so <code>len(set(ids)) == len(ids)</code> is the one-line duplicate check, and <code>set(prediction) == set(reference)</code> compares key sets. A list comprehension, <code>[r["id"] for r in rows if r["status"] == "scored"]</code>, replaces most loops that build a list. Sorting is <code>sorted(rows, key=lambda r: r["id"])</code>, stable, and non-mutating.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p><code>summarize_run</code> must raise for a duplicate terminal result rather than count it twice. The alternative, silently keeping one, changes the denominator without changing the number of rows anyone looks at. The sibling statistics course calls that an inclusion-policy change; here it is a loader bug.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p><code>except Exception: pass</code>. It turns a bad line into a silently shorter dataset, and the eval runs on fewer cases than were scheduled. If you must continue past a bad record, record where and why, and report the count.</p></div>''',
 'A loader reads 1,000 lines, reports 998 cases, and raises nothing. Name two ways that could happen with the code you have seen, and what each does to the eval.',
 'Two blank lines, which is fine and should be visible as line numbers skipping. Or two records with duplicate ids kept by a dict keyed on id, which silently replaces earlier cases with later ones; or two malformed lines swallowed by a broad except. The first changes nothing. The others change which cases are graded while the headline count looks plausible, which is why the spec requires raising with the line number.',
 lab('Write a function <code>reject_duplicate_keys(pairs)</code> like the one in the script, then call <code>json.loads(text, object_pairs_hook=reject_duplicate_keys)</code> on an object whose nested <code>"reference"</code> object repeats <code>"urgent"</code>. Confirm it raises for the nested duplicate too, then use it inside <code>load_cases</code>.',
 'The hook runs for every object literal at every depth, so one function covers both the duplicate at the top level and the duplicate inside <code>"reference"</code>. Wrap the <code>ValueError</code> it raises so the message carries the physical line number; the hook itself cannot know it.',
 ['python3 labs/examples/records_and_errors.py']), ['py-json'], 'dict list json jsonl exceptions try except raise ValueError FileNotFoundError'),

lesson(4,1,'spec-first-testing','Spec-first testing with unittest: the three eval-pipeline labs',
 'You already write the test first. Here is how that habit looks in Python, using the three functions whose tests are waiting for you.',
 '''<h2 id="unittest">unittest in five lines</h2>
<p>A test is a method starting with <code>test_</code> on a class that subclasses <code>unittest.TestCase</code>. Assertions are methods: <code>assertEqual</code>, <code>assertIs</code>, <code>assertTrue</code>, <code>assertRaises</code>. Discovery runs every file matching a pattern under a start directory: <code>python3 -m unittest discover -s labs -p 'test_*.py'</code>, where <code>-s</code> is the start directory and <code>-p</code> the file pattern '''+cite('py-unittest')+'''. Add <code>-v</code> to see each test's name and result. There is no fixture magic to learn beyond <code>setUp</code>, which runs before each test, and <code>setUpClass</code>, which runs once per class and is where expensive things like loading GPT-2 belong.</p>
'''+code('''import unittest
import shapes

class RowNormalizeTests(unittest.TestCase):
    def test_zero_rows_stay_zero_without_nan(self):
        x = np.array([[3.0, 4.0], [0.0, 0.0]])
        np.testing.assert_allclose(shapes.row_normalize(x), [[0.6, 0.8], [0.0, 0.0]])

    def test_mismatch_raises_value_error(self):
        with self.assertRaises(ValueError):
            shapes.broadcast_shape((4, 3), (4,))''')+'''
<p>Two idioms matter more than the rest. <code>assertRaises</code> as a context manager wraps exactly the call that should fail, so a typo on the line before cannot pass the test by accident. And <code>subTest</code> labels each case of a parameterised loop, so when the loop fails you are told which input did it rather than just that the first one did. The eval-pipeline tests use both, heavily.</p>
<h2 id="the-labs">The three functions and what their tests say</h2>
<p>The spec is <a href="../labs/ASSESSMENTS.md">labs/ASSESSMENTS.md</a> and the tests are <code>dag-tests/test_python_evals.py</code> at the repository root. Read the tests as the specification they are, the way you would read a Rust test module before touching the implementation.</p>
<p><strong><code>score_ticket(prediction, reference) -> bool</code></strong>. A malformed reference is an evaluation defect and raises <code>ValueError</code>; a malformed prediction is a candidate failure and returns <code>False</code>. The pass condition is exact: same key set, and for each key the same type and the same value. The tests include <code>reference(urgent=1)</code>, which is <code>==</code> to the reference and must still fail, and <code>reference(category="refund ")</code>, which is not trimmed. Return <code>True</code> and <code>False</code> themselves: <code>assertIs</code> rejects a truthy <code>1</code>.</p>
<p><strong><code>load_cases(path) -> list[dict]</code></strong>. Read UTF-8 JSONL, validate every record and its nested reference, reject duplicate JSON keys at any depth and duplicate case ids, and raise <code>ValueError</code> with <code>line N</code> in the message, where N is the physical line. Keep <code>FileNotFoundError</code> distinct. Everything you need was in lesson 03: <code>enumerate(f, start=1)</code>, <code>object_pairs_hook</code>, and the type checks.</p>
<p><strong><code>summarize_run(scheduled_ids, results) -> dict</code></strong>. Account for every scheduled case as scored, errored, or missing; counts are <code>int</code>, rates are <code>float</code> or <code>None</code> when the denominator is zero; duplicates and unknown ids raise. The tests check <code>type(result["scored"]) is int</code>, so do not let a <code>numpy.int64</code> or a <code>bool</code> leak in, and check the identities <code>passed + failed == scored</code> and <code>scored + errors + missing == scheduled</code> hold for your output before the tests do.</p>
<h2 id="red-first">Run them red first</h2>
<p>From the repository root. The stubs raise <code>NotImplementedError</code>, so every test errors. That is the starting state, and the last three lines of the output are the ones to watch as you work.</p>
'''+run("python3 -m unittest discover -s dag-tests -p 'test_python_evals.py' 2>&1 | tail -3", cwd='../..')+'''
<p>Work one class at a time: <code>ScorerTests</code>, then <code>LoaderTests</code>, then <code>ReportingTests</code>. Run a single class with <code>python3 -m unittest dag-tests.test_python_evals.ScorerTests</code> from the root if your layout allows it, or simply filter by eye with <code>-v</code>. Each time a test goes green, read its name again and ask whether your code passes it for the stated reason or by coincidence.</p>
<h2 id="this-course">The same pattern in this course's labs</h2>
<p>The labs under <code>labs/</code> follow the same rule: a stub, a test file, and a spec section in <code>ASSESSMENTS.md</code>. The shape lab is red now too, and will stay red until lesson 07.</p>
'''+run("python3 -m unittest discover -s labs -p 'test_shapes.py' 2>&1 | tail -3")+'''
<p>One habit from spec-first work in any language transfers unchanged: when a test is hard to make pass, the test is telling you about the design. <code>summarize_run</code> is easier with a <code>Counter</code> and a set of seen ids than with nested ifs, and the "do not mutate inputs" tests are trivially satisfied by never writing to the arguments.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p>The three functions are an eval runner in miniature: a scorer with an explicit policy, a loader that refuses bad data loudly, and a report that keeps failures in the denominator. Every eval harness you will meet has the same three parts, and the same three ways to be quietly wrong.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Reading the test, writing code that mirrors the test's example data, and calling it done. The held-out variants a tutor will give you change the schema, the loader, and the denominator; the published tests are practice, and <code>ASSESSMENTS.md</code> says so.</p></div>''',
 '<code>test_python_equal_values_can_still_have_wrong_types</code> first asserts that <code>reference(urgent=1) == reference()</code>. Why does a test assert something about Python before asserting anything about your code?',
 'It documents the trap in the test itself: the equality that would make a naive implementation pass is real, so a reader who sees the test later understands why the strict type check exists. A test that encodes the reason for a rule is worth more than a comment, because it fails if the reason stops being true.',
 lab('Implement <code>score_ticket</code> only, run the scorer tests, then write one additional test of your own for a false pass that plain dict equality would admit and your implementation rejects. Keep it in a separate file so the published tests stay unchanged.',
 'A good extra test is <code>order_id=42</code> against <code>"42"</code> (type), or a key set that is a superset (extra field). The exercise is to state the rule in test form before writing the code that enforces it; the spec asks you to explain exactly such a case to a tutor.',
 [dict(command="python3 -m unittest discover -s dag-tests -p 'test_python_evals.py' 2>&1 | tail -3", cwd='../..')]), ['py-unittest'], 'unittest discover assertRaises subTest score_ticket load_cases summarize_run spec'),

lesson(5,1,'functions-generators-dataclasses','Functions, generators, dataclasses, and type hints as documentation',
 'The idioms you will read in every toolkit: keyword arguments, lazy iteration, records as classes, and hints that document without enforcing.',
 '''<h2 id="functions">Functions and the calling conventions you will read</h2>
<p>Python functions take positional and keyword arguments, and most library calls you will read use keywords for anything optional: <code>model(**enc, output_hidden_states=True)</code>. The <code>**enc</code> unpacks a dict into keyword arguments, so a tokenizer's output dict with <code>input_ids</code> and <code>attention_mask</code> becomes two named arguments. <code>*args</code> collects extra positionals into a tuple and <code>**kwargs</code> extra keywords into a dict; a forward hook's signature, <code>hook(module, args, output)</code>, receives the module's positional inputs as that tuple.</p>
<p>Functions are objects. You pass them to <code>register_forward_hook</code>, store them in dicts, and return them from other functions, which is how lesson 13 builds one hook per layer with a closure: <code>make_hook(name)</code> returns a function that remembers <code>name</code>. A <code>lambda</code> is a one-expression anonymous function; fine for a sort key, cramped for a hook.</p>
'''+run('python3 labs/examples/generators_and_dataclasses.py')+'''
<h2 id="generators">Generators: a Stream without the module</h2>
<p>A function containing <code>yield</code> returns a generator object when called and runs nothing until something asks for a value. <code>read_jsonl</code> above opened no file at the call; <code>next(rows)</code> read one line. The rest were consumed by a comprehension, and after that the generator is exhausted: <code>list()</code> of the same generator object would be empty, which is why the script calls <code>read_jsonl(path)</code> again. If you know Elixir's <code>Stream</code> or Rust's iterators, the mental model is the same: lazy, single-pass, and composable with <code>for</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>any</code>, and comprehensions.</p>
<p>The generator expression <code>(n * n for n in range(4))</code> is the inline form. The million-term sum builds no list. Use generators for files larger than memory and for activations you want to process one batch at a time; use lists when you need to index, take <code>len</code>, or iterate twice.</p>
<h2 id="dataclasses">Dataclasses: records with a generated constructor</h2>
<p><code>@dataclass</code> reads the annotated fields and generates <code>__init__</code>, <code>__repr__</code>, and a field-by-field <code>__eq__</code> '''+cite('py-dataclasses')+'''. The printed <code>Result(...)</code> is the generated repr; the equality line is the generated <code>__eq__</code>. <code>frozen=True</code> makes assignment raise <code>FrozenInstanceError</code>, which is as close to an Elixir struct as Python gets, and the right default for records that go into a log.</p>
<p>The <code>Run</code> class shows the module refusing a list default, by design: a mutable default would be shared across instances, the trap from lesson 02. <code>field(default_factory=list)</code> calls <code>list()</code> per instance, and the output shows the two runs with separate lists. Hugging Face's <code>ModelOutput</code> classes are dataclasses too, which is why <code>out.logits</code> and <code>out.hidden_states</code> are attributes with generated reprs.</p>
<h2 id="hints">Type hints document; they do not check</h2>
<p><code>def accuracy(passed: int, scored: int) -> float | None</code> tells a reader what to pass and what comes back. It tells the interpreter nothing. The last lines show <code>accuracy("3", "4")</code> failing only because <code>/</code> refuses strings, not because the hint said <code>int</code>. Tools such as <code>mypy</code> or <code>pyright</code> check hints statically if you run them; this course does not require them, but writing hints on every function you share is the cheapest documentation there is, and <code>list[str]</code>, <code>dict[str, float]</code>, and <code>X | None</code> cover most of what you need. For tensors, a hint cannot express a shape, which is why the convention is a comment: <code># (batch, seq, d_model)</code> next to the parameter. Treat that comment as part of the signature.</p>
<h2 id="context-managers">Context managers</h2>
<p><code>with open(path) as f:</code> closes the file when the block exits, even on an exception. <code>with torch.no_grad():</code> turns gradient recording off for the block. <code>with model.trace(prompt):</code> in nnsight captures a block of code to run against a forward pass. The protocol is two methods, <code>__enter__</code> and <code>__exit__</code>, and <code>contextlib.contextmanager</code> lets you write one from a generator with a single <code>yield</code>, which is the neatest way to write "register these hooks, run this, remove them no matter what" in lesson 13.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p>A frozen dataclass for a terminal result, with <code>status</code> and either <code>passed</code> or <code>error</code>, makes the invalid states in <code>summarize_run</code>'s tests, such as an error row that also carries <code>passed</code>, impossible to construct by accident. Validate once at the boundary, then trust the type.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Iterating a generator twice. The second loop is silently empty, and a metric computed over it is zero over zero. If a function might be called on a generator, convert with <code>list()</code> at the entry or document that it consumes its input.</p></div>''',
 'A colleague annotates a function <code>def cache(acts: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor</code> and it is called with a tuple of tensors from a hook. Nothing warns. What happens, and what should the signature have said?',
 'Nothing warns because hints are not enforced; the function proceeds until some tensor method is called on a tuple and raises <code>AttributeError</code> several lines later. GPT-2 block outputs in this transformers version are tensors, but other modules return tuples, so the hint should admit the possibility or the hook should normalise with <code>output[0] if isinstance(output, tuple) else output</code> first, which lesson 13 does.',
 lab('Rewrite <code>load_cases</code> as a generator <code>iter_cases(path)</code> that yields validated records one at a time, then implement <code>load_cases</code> as <code>list(iter_cases(path))</code>. Confirm the loader tests still pass and that the duplicate-id test still raises with the correct line number.',
 'Duplicate detection needs state across yields, a set of seen ids kept inside the generator, which is fine because a generator keeps its locals alive between yields. The line-number message comes from the same <code>enumerate(f, start=1)</code>, and the exception propagates out of <code>list()</code> unchanged.',
 ['python3 labs/examples/generators_and_dataclasses.py']), ['py-dataclasses','py-unittest'], 'functions kwargs closures generators yield dataclass frozen type hints context manager'),
]
